---
const published = '2026-09-14';
const updated = '2026-11-20';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = 'Architecture Marketing Budget Calculator: Ad Spend to Booked Consultations | IPQmedia';
const description =
  'Put in a monthly ad budget and see the leads and booked consultations it bought across 26 US architecture and design firm ad accounts over twelve months. Median $51.50 per lead, $177 per booked consultation, split by residential and commercial.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} published={published} updated={updated}>
  <h1>Architecture marketing budget calculator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every agency you have spoken to has an opinion about what your firm should spend, and not one
    of them will show you the arithmetic underneath it. This will. Put in a monthly number and it
    returns what that budget bought across 26 US architecture and design firm ad accounts over a
    full year, as a range, because the honest answer is a range.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>
      Across 26 US architecture and design firm ad accounts running $161,236 of Meta spend in the
      twelve months to 17 November 2026, the median account paid $51.50 per lead and $177.19 per
      booked consultation. A $3,000 month at those figures is roughly 58 leads and 17 booked
      consultations, in a realistic band of 11 to 29 consultations depending on where your account
      lands in the spread. Residential and commercial firms pay different prices for a lead and
      book them at different rates, so choose your segment before you read the number.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The calculator</h2>
  <p>
    Four inputs, because four things in our data actually moved the price. Every figure comes from
    the per-account distribution in the November edition of the <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026">2026 architecture marketing benchmark report</a>, our own ad account data rather than a survey of opinions.
  </p>

  <div class="calc" id="calc">
    <div class="calc-grid">
      <label class="calc-f"><span>Monthly ad budget</span>
        <input type="number" id="c-budget" value="3000" min="200" max="200000" step="100" />
      </label>
      <label class="calc-f"><span>Who your firm works for</span>
        <select id="c-seg">
          <option value="all" selected>All firms in the data (n=24)</option>
          <option value="res">Residential (n=8)</option>
          <option value="com">Commercial (n=2)</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label class="calc-f"><span>Where the lead is captured</span>
        <select id="c-cap">
          <option value="mixed" selected>Mixed, as most accounts run</option>
          <option value="form">In-platform form</option>
          <option value="landing">Your own landing page</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label class="calc-f"><span>Quarter you are planning</span>
        <select id="c-season">
          <option value="year" selected>Full-year average</option>
          <option value="q1">January to March</option>
          <option value="q2">April to June</option>
          <option value="q3">July to September</option>
          <option value="q4">October to December</option>
        </select>
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="calc-out">
      <div class="calc-tile"><span class="calc-n" id="o-leads">58</span><span class="calc-l">leads a month</span></div>
      <div class="calc-tile"><span class="calc-n" id="o-book">17</span><span class="calc-l">booked consultations a month</span></div>
      <div class="calc-tile"><span class="calc-n" id="o-cpb">$177</span><span class="calc-l">per booked consultation</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="calc-band">Middle-of-the-pack account. The middle half of our accounts landed between <b id="o-lo">11</b> and <b id="o-hi">29</b> booked consultations on that budget.</p>
    <table class="calc-ladder">
      <tr><th>Monthly budget</th><th>Leads</th><th>Booked consultations</th><th>Middle half</th></tr>
      <tr><td data-b="1500">$1,500</td><td class="l-lead">29</td><td class="l-book">8</td><td class="l-band">5 to 15</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-b="3000">$3,000</td><td class="l-lead">58</td><td class="l-book">17</td><td class="l-band">11 to 29</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-b="5000">$5,000</td><td class="l-lead">97</td><td class="l-book">28</td><td class="l-band">18 to 49</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-b="10000">$10,000</td><td class="l-lead">194</td><td class="l-book">56</td><td class="l-band">36 to 98</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="calc-note">Ad spend only, before anyone's fee. The four settings were measured separately, so stacking the best of each gives you a ceiling rather than a forecast. The ladder scales in a straight line, and real budget raises did not (see below).</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What these numbers are, and what they are not</h2>
  <p>
    The population is 26 US architecture and design firm accounts we ran between 18 November 2025
    and 17 November 2026: $161,236 of spend, 2,460,210 impressions, 3,427 leads and 1,187 booked
    consultations. Two construction contractors and one engineering firm in the same tables were
    excluded as a different business.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each account counts once, not each dollar, and the 24 accounts with at least ten leads carry
    the medians. That choice earned its keep in September, when a few account days reported more
    link clicks than total clicks and dragged a pooled cost per link click down to $1.45 against
    $3.60 per account. This pull still had nine of those days, so click figures exclude them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The spread between accounts is the finding, not the median. Cost per lead ran from $12.58 to
    $88.33, about seven to one, on the same channel in the same year. Any tool that hands you one
    confident number is quietly not telling you that.
  </p>

  <h2>What moved when we re-based the calculator</h2>
  <p>
    This page ran on the September pull until today. Re-basing it on the November edition moved
    the headline output down, and the reason is worth more than the number.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Figure</th><th>September (21 accounts)</th><th>November (26 accounts)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Median cost per lead</td><td>$50.41</td><td>$51.50</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Median cost per booked consultation</td><td>$147.76</td><td>$177.19</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Middle half, per booked consultation</td><td>$104.78 to $239.57</td><td>$102.05 to $276.76</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Form lead against landing page lead</td><td>$40.55 against $70.18</td><td>$41.84 against $67.96</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cheapest and dearest quarter</td><td>0.87 (Jan to Mar), 1.12 (Apr to Jun)</td><td>0.91 (Jan to Mar), 1.13 (Oct to Dec)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>$3,000 a month, booked consultations</td><td>20 (13 to 29)</td><td>17 (11 to 29)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    The price of a lead barely moved. What moved is the booking side of the median firm. Six
    accounts launched after September, and the five with enough leads to count sit at a median of
    $198 per booked consultation against $126 for the rest. Part of that is timing, because a
    booking trails its lead and those accounts have had weeks rather than months for bookings to
    land. The top of the band stayed at 29. The bottom fell, and that is where a new account sits
    in its first months.
  </p>

  <h2>Residential and commercial pay different prices for the same consultation</h2>
  <p>
    Ten of the 26 accounts carry a recorded market segment, eight residential against two
    commercial. Two accounts is a direction rather than a benchmark, and saying so beats letting a
    thin bucket pass as a finding. With buckets this small the segment rows are pooled across their
    accounts, while the all-firms setting is the median account, so the segment settings read
    cheaper than the default.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Residential (n=8)</th><th>Commercial (n=2)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost per lead</td><td>$41.51</td><td>$46.25</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Leads that became a booked consultation</td><td>38.3%</td><td>51.9%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost per booked consultation</td><td>$108.47</td><td>$89.09</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost per thousand impressions</td><td>$60.07</td><td>$61.74</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    A commercial lead cost about 11% more, on reach that cost about the same. Then it books at
    51.9% against 38.3% and the order flips: commercial lands at $89 per booked consultation
    against $108 for residential, the number that matters, the cost of getting someone real into
    the calendar.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the common advice that paid social is a residential channel does not survive contact with
    our own accounts. The play is different rather than weaker. Residential creative sells the
    finished house to someone imagining living in it. Commercial creative sells a delivered project
    to a developer or business owner reading it as risk and return, and that buyer takes longer to
    decide, which is what retargeting is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both are the same channel, covered in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>, and each gets its own treatment in <a href="/marketing-for-residential-architects">marketing for residential architects</a> and <a href="/marketing-for-commercial-architects">marketing for commercial architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The biggest lever is not the budget, it is where the lead is captured</h2>
  <p>
    36 campaigns across 20 accounts collected the lead in an in-platform form and paid $41.84 per
    lead on $70,415 of spend. Eleven campaigns across eight accounts sent the click to the firm's
    own landing page and paid $67.96 on $41,928. Same channel, same year, same kind of firm, and a
    62% difference in what a lead cost.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why the capture setting moves this calculator more than the season does. 72% of every
    lead in the dataset arrived through a form the client never had to leave. The trade is real: a
    form buys volume, your own page buys a more committed inquiry and a chance to qualify before
    the call. The page you would be sending them to is the subject of <a href="/website-design-for-architects">website design for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What month you start changes the price</h2>
  <p>
    Index every account month against that same account's own twelve-month cost per lead, so
    market and offer cancel out, and the price of a lead moves with the calendar. At the middle
    account month, January to March sits at 0.91 (10 account months, four accounts), April to June
    at 1.08 (14, six accounts), July to September at 0.92 (15, eight) and October to December at
    1.13 (10, eight).
  </p>
  <p>
    The calculator uses the middle account month rather than the average. Three expensive spring
    months pulled the April to June average up to 1.47, and a planning tool should not hand one bad
    May to every firm. Restricted to account months with at least 20 days of spend and five leads.
    Two caveats: only four accounts advertised through the winter, and we measured this on the cost
    of a lead, not the rate at which leads book, so the consultation figure inherits the assumption
    rather than proving it.
  </p>

  <h2>Does doubling the budget double the consultations?</h2>
  <p>
    Not in our accounts, and the firms asking already suspect it. One owner put it to us on a sales
    call in July 2026: "It seems like we're in a diminishing marginal return where 200 per day isn't
    going to get you twice as much as 100." The calculator above scales in a straight line, so we
    went looking for what actually happened when a firm raised its budget.
  </p>
  <p>
    We scanned every architecture and design account from 1 October 2025 to 5 November 2026 for a
    raise: four weeks averaging at least 40% more spend than the four weeks before, both windows
    live for 24 days or more, and at least five leads before the raise to compare against. Five
    accounts made one.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Raise</th><th>Daily spend, before to after</th><th>Leads in four weeks</th><th>Cost per lead</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Late October 2025</td><td>$51 to $74</td><td>19 to 20</td><td>$74.51 to $103.52</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late October 2025</td><td>$48 to $71</td><td>28 to 37</td><td>$48.22 to $53.93</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late January 2026</td><td>$27 to $41</td><td>17 to 18</td><td>$44.13 to $64.44</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mid February 2026</td><td>$28 to $49</td><td>12 to 22</td><td>$66.45 to $62.14</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Early August 2026</td><td>$63 to $107</td><td>87 to 76</td><td>$20.33 to $39.26</td></tr>
    <tr><td>All five together</td><td>$6,082 to $9,576 in four weeks</td><td>163 to 173</td><td>$37.31 to $55.35</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Together the five spent 57% more and got 6% more leads. The price of a lead rose in four of the
    five, by a median of 39%. Only one account, the February raise, bought roughly what the extra
    money should have bought. The calendar does not explain it either: each raise sits almost
    entirely inside a single quarter of the seasonal index above.
  </p>
  <p>
    Five accounts is a pattern, not a law. Our read is that a raise mostly pushes more money through
    the same ads to the same audience, and new money needs new creative and often a wider audience
    to buy at the old price. So read the $5,000 and $10,000 rows of the calculator as
    a ceiling on day one, and expect to earn your way back toward it over the following months.
  </p>

  <h2>What is the minimum ad budget for an architecture firm?</h2>
  <p>
    Lower than the usual advice says. That advice is built on Meta's learning phase, and <a href="https://www.facebook.com/business/help/112167992830700">Meta's own help page on it</a> (read 4 November 2026) says an ad set exits learning "after about 50 results in the week after the ad set's last significant edit." At our median $51.50 a lead, that is about $2,600 a week, or roughly $370 a day, for every ad set.
  </p>
  <p>
    Almost none of our accounts ever got there. Across the 24 accounts in the twelve months to 5
    November 2026, the typical one averaged $79 a day, and exactly one of 350 account weeks produced
    50 leads, with every ad set in the account added together. The medians this whole page is built
    on came from accounts running below the threshold the whole year.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the real floor is not the algorithm, it is your own patience with small numbers. Another
    owner asked us in October 2026: "Is 50 bucks a day going to take us a year to get a good
    project versus a thousand a day?" At the median, $50 a day is about 30 leads and 9 booked
    consultations a month, with the first lead usually inside a day. Below about $1,000 a month you
    get so few leads that a slow month and a weak offer look identical.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this stops at the booked consultation</h2>
  <p>
    Because that is where our data stops being trustworthy. Of the 1,187 bookings in this pull, 97
    were later canceled and only 6 carry a recorded outcome of held or no-show. A show rate computed
    off that would be a number about our record keeping, and a projection of signed projects built
    on top of it would be fiction with a dollar sign in front.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the calculator hands you a booked consultation and stops. What happens on that call is your
    sales process, your fee and your close rate, the part of the funnel an ad account cannot see.
    The standards this page is held to are written down in <a href="/editorial-standards">our editorial standards</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more real-world anchor. A median full month in this dataset ran $1,529 of spend and 24
    leads across 42 complete account months. Those are two separate medians, so dividing one by the
    other does not give you a cost per lead, but it is the closest thing to an ordinary month here.
    Worth knowing alongside it: 828 of 2,253 live account days produced zero leads, so the monthly
    figure is the honest unit and a quiet Tuesday means nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>How much should an architecture firm spend on marketing a month?</h3>
  <p>
    Work backwards from consultations rather than forwards from a percentage. Decide how many
    qualified calls a month your firm can actually take, multiply by roughly $180, and that is your
    ad budget before anyone's fee. Then pick a number you can hold for three months, because a
    budget cut off after eight weeks has only just told you which end of the spread you sit on.
  </p>
  <h3>What does a lead cost for an architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    A median of $51.50 across 24 accounts over twelve months, with the middle half between $30.30
    and $68.96 and the full range from $12.58 to $88.33. For context, LocaliQ's Facebook
    advertising benchmarks, 2026 edition, last updated 23 September 2026, put the all-industry
    average at $27.39 and home improvement at $42.95. Neither of those rows is architecture, which is the gap this
    dataset exists to fill.
  </p>
  <h3>How many consultations will $5,000 a month get me?</h3>
  <p>
    On the all-accounts figures, about 97 leads and 28 booked consultations, with the middle half of
    accounts between 18 and 49. Where you land depends far more on your offer, creative and
    follow-up speed than on the budget. A firm at the expensive end of our middle half gets a bit
    over a third of what a firm at the cheap end gets from identical spend.
  </p>
  <h3>If I double my ad budget, will I get twice the leads?</h3>
  <p>
    Probably not at first. In the five budget raises in our data, spend went up 57% and leads went
    up 6%, and the cost of a lead rose in four of the five. Raise in steps, add new creative with
    each step, and judge the new level after four weeks rather than four days.
  </p>
  <h3>Why is the range so wide?</h3>
  <p>
    Because it genuinely is, at about seven to one between our cheapest and most expensive account.
    A single confident figure would make the tool feel more authoritative and the answer worse,
    which is the trade every marketing calculator on the internet quietly takes.
  </p>
  <h3>Does this work for a commercial firm?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, with the sample size stated out loud. Only two accounts in this cut carry a commercial
    segment, so treat those rows as a direction rather than a benchmark. The direction is
    encouraging: commercial leads cost about 11% more to generate and book at a much higher rate,
    so the cost per booked consultation came in below residential.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do with the number</h2>
  <p>
    Treat it as a planning floor, not a promise. If the band says 11 to 29 consultations, forecast
    against the bottom of it, then spend the first 60 days finding out which end of the spread your
    firm sits on. That is a fact about your offer and your market rather than about the channel,
    and you learn it fast: time to first lead had a median of one day, and 20 of the 21 accounts
    launched in the window that produced a lead saw one inside week one. Once the budget is settled, the sequence it funds is set out in the <a href="/marketing-plan-for-architects">marketing plan for an architecture firm</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full dataset, including the findings this year's editions retired, is in the <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026">benchmark report</a>, the wider cost picture sits in <a href="/architecture-marketing-statistics-and-costs-2026">architecture marketing statistics and costs</a>, and how these numbers fit the rest of the machine is mapped in <a href="/architecture-marketing">architecture marketing</a> and <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects">lead generation for architects</a>. If you are weighing who carries the risk on a budget like this, that is <a href="/pay-per-lead-vs-retainer-for-architects">pay per lead against retainer</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    We run this for architecture firms on a pay-per-performance basis, which means your budget funds
    the ads and we get paid when a qualified consultation actually shows up. Same numbers, our risk.
  </p>
